  "text": "Five husbands, and the man at the well already knew. He told her who he was anyway. She is the first person he says it to outright. John four. Jesus is tired. Sits down by a well. About the sixth hour, so, midday heat. A woman of Samaria comes to draw water. He says, \"Give me to drink.\" That is two scandals in one sentence, and she names them both herself: \"How is it that thou, being a Jew, askest drink of me, which am a woman of Samaria?\" Then the conversation turns. He offers her living water. She wants it. He says, go call your husband. She says, \"I have no husband.\" And he goes, \"Thou hast well said, I have no husband.\" Then: \"For thou hast had five husbands; and he whom thou now hast is not thy husband.\" He is not dragging her. He is telling her he already knew and he is still sitting there. Now, people love to say she came at noon to dodge the gossip. That is a reasonable guess. It is still a guess. The text just says the sixth hour. Here is what the text does say. She brings up the Messiah. And he answers, \"I that speak unto thee am he.\" That is the first time in this Gospel he says it outright. To a Samaritan woman with five marriages behind her, at a well, at midday. And she left her waterpot. Left it. And went and told the town.",
